API BASED QUESTIONS
1)🌐 What is a REST API?
A REST API allows a client (like a browser or mobile app) to talk to a server (like a database backend) and perform operations like:
Get data
Send data
Update data
Delete data
🌐Key Principles of REST
✅ Uses HTTP
✅ Stateless (Server doesn’t remember previous request)
✅ Uses resources (like users, products)
✅ Usually returns JSON
🌐REST Uses HTTP Methods
These are the main methods used:
Method	Meaning	     Example
GET	    Read data    Get all users
POST	  Create data	 Add new user
PUT   	Update data	 Update user info
DELETE	Delete data	 Remove user

2)What is the difference between API and REST API?
1️⃣ What is an API?
API (Application Programming Interface) is a general term.
👉 It is any method that allows two software applications to communicate with each other.
It does not have to use HTTP.
It can be:
Library API (like Java API)
OS API (Windows API)
Database API
Web API
So basically:
API = A way for programs to talk to each other.
2️⃣ What is a REST API?
A REST API is a type of API that:
Uses HTTP
Follows REST architecture rules
Works with resources (like users, products, orders)
Uses methods like GET, POST, PUT, DELETE
Usually returns JSON
So:
REST API = API that follows REST principles over HTTP.

3)What do you mean by RESTful web services?
A RESTful web service:
✅ Uses HTTP protocol
✅ Uses resource-based URLs
✅ Uses HTTP methods properly
✅ Returns data (usually JSON)
✅ Is stateless

REST vs RESTful
REST = Architectural style (rules)
RESTful = An API that follows those rules
Q)MESSAGING IN RESTFUL WEB SERVICE
Messaging in RESTful web services is the process of sending and receiving structured HTTP request
and response messages between client and server to access or modify resources.

Q) Explain ‘Addressing’ in RESTful web services
Addressing in RESTful web services refers to the way resources are uniquely identified and accessed 
using Uniform Resource Identifiers (URIs).
In simple words:
👉 Addressing means how we locate a resource on the server using a URL.
REST uses URIs (Uniform Resource Identifiers) to identify resources.
Example:
http://example.com/users
Here:
users is the resource
This URI represents all users

Q) Why are REST services easily scalable?
REST services are easily scalable due to several inherent characteristics of the RESTful architecture. Here are the key reasons why REST services are scalable:

Stateless Communication: RESTful services follow a stateless communication model, allowing requests to be processed independently and in parallel.
Resource-Oriented Design: REST APIs are centered around resources, allowing individual resources to be scaled independently.
Cacheability: RESTful services support caching, thereby reducing the load on the server by reusing stored responses.
Load Balancing: Load balancers distribute incoming requests across multiple servers, thus ensuring an even workload distribution.
Horizontal Scaling: REST services can be horizontally scaled by adding more servers or instances to handle increased demand.
Cloud Infrastructure: REST services can leverage cloud platforms with auto-scaling capabilities for efficient resource allocation.

Q)Differentiate between POST and PUT methods.
POST Method
This method is used to build new server resources.
This method is not idempotent. Hence, multiple identical requests may result in different outcomes.
The POST Method is typically used for submitting data to create a new resource.
In this method, the server processes the request, creates a new resource, and responds with a representation of the created resource.

PUT Method
This method is used to update or replace an existing resource on the server or to create a resource if one doesn’t exist.
This method is idempotent. Hence, sending the same request multiple times will have the same outcome.
The PUT method is typically used for updating or replacing an entire resource.

Q)What is CRUD?
CRUD stands for:
C – Create
R – Read
U – Update
D – Delete
It represents the four basic operations that can be performed on data in a database or system.
